<h2>A Family of Her Own</h2>
Image here floated right
<p>Seven years later, William Webster Bates applied for a license to marry Minnie Staggs — the record shows a marriage date a year later than expected{% cite "minnie-staggs-marriage-1887" %}, most likely a clerking error rather than a second ceremony; see <a href="/people/florence-staggs/">Florence's page</a> for the full discrepancy. By 1900, Minnie and her husband appear on the Federal Census with their four children{% cite "minnie-1900-census-oklahoma" %} — see <a href="/people/florence-staggs/">Florence's page</a> for the full family list. The Census was conducted in July of that year, so there was no record of their son, Chancy, who was born August 22 but died 12 days later.{% cite "louis-cemetery-chancy-bates" %} At this time, William and Minnie own their home and farm free and clear. Minnie has not learned to write, but the census indicates that she can now read.</p>
<p>Image Here</p>
<p>In 1910, Minnie and William are renting a farm in Looney, Oklahoma, and have added three more children and one grandchild to their household{% cite "florence-bates-1910-census" %} — see <a href="/people/florence-staggs/">Florence's page</a> for the full family list. This is the last federal census that Nora appeared in as she will die in 1918,{% cite "louis-cemetery-nora-bates" %} reportedly a victim of the 1918 influenza pandemic (although I have found no official records to support the cause of death). On this census, Minnie is reported as a white female who can read and write.</p>
<p>Image Here</p>
<h3>William W. and Minnie Bates</h3>
<p>William makes his last appearance on a Federal census in 1920,{% cite "minnie-bates-1920-census" %} and will pass away four years before the next census. Sometime after William's death in 1926, Minnie moves to Los Angeles, California, presumably to live near her daughter, Ada (now called Edythe), who appears on the 1930 Federal Census in Los Angeles.{% cite "haman-1930-census-los-angeles" %} I have not been able to locate Minnie on a census after 1920, but her death certificate{% cite "minnie-bates-birth-death-index-1935" %} indicates she owned a home in Los Angeles and had been a resident for 10 years.</p>
<p>Minnie died of cerebral hemorrhage in Los Angeles — see <a href="/people/florence-staggs/">her page</a> for the full detail of her final illness. In an interesting final twist, the funeral director noted the body was originally to be transported to Quanah, Texas, but she was ultimately buried beside her husband in Harmon County, Oklahoma.{% cite "louis-cemetery-minnie-ww-bates" %}</p>
